8b" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543679 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543680 1071 "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543680 1072 #uuid "06448e69-2e5e-5172-aee5-b3151a22cc6e" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543680 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/4f/4ff66220bf03e9c5dde372e20aa060fa26a4a57ab481a61845726fde8ca25046.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta-2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta-4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/88/882f7487d40a724c331aebb3dd954b8beace88ff5a2e65b4b2b9abedd74885dc.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=\\pi.$\n\n![](images/41/410279d76b3d76135133c63fe19003839b783a5d4177aff6744bd950fcbe93bd.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543681 1080 "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543681 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543681 1083 #uuid "0afc24b7-4e9b-5fc1-a813-41887ebcebbf" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543682 1080 "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543682 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543682 1083 #uuid "0ffe947f-52e1-584d-be76-77ece40d3f41" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543683 1080 "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543683 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543683 1083 #uuid "154fe4ca-a591-51af-b5a1-03adc5261ae6" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543684 1080 "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543684 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543684 1083 #uuid "15cbb851-3dfc-5f92-872