43704 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/866f3162cd881e997f28cea68fe22fc90b8ef011b6d8beff00ef38d9a69d2eb9.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+y^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-2)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-4r\\cos\\theta+4+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/6c/6c841a1dc73b8a68ba8d51bb4d0f8d1ff051f8d1c0bafdfdbfac134ccbc651aa.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/08/083360143d05d210dcbd11ed86c3a5cc0f2510beeecb68a210e9f52632609867.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543705 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543705 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543705 1083 #uuid "7ee5d5f6-e473-541e-87b4-e95658ad2cff" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543706 1080 "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543706 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543706 1083 #uuid "86580f35-f153-55b3-b4bc-2e64e0c0b121" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186543718 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543707 1071 "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x+2)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543707 1072 #uuid "9021eafc-a91a-5e03-85b9-0baff1b49b29" 13194139535926 17592186045443 true] [17592186543707 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3a/3a77e854cc9553675752e3e4e231c8915d390091a4f4fa22cee2eb5ffe279c3e.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(-2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\the